Exactly—a cartel can develop a cult around itself, and a cult can operate like a cartel.

They begin from different centers:

* A cartel coordinates control of a market, territory, resource, or opportunity.
* A cult coordinates control of meaning, identity, loyalty, and perceived reality.

But each can discover the other’s machinery. A cartel builds mythology, rituals, sacred leaders, martyr stories, silence codes, symbols, and identity fusion. A cult may monopolize relationships, information, labor, money, sex, salvation, or access to status.

The hybrid becomes a coercive meaning monopoly:

\text{control resources}
+
\text{control interpretation}
+
\text{punish exit}
=
\text{self-reinforcing power}

That last part is diagnostic. Healthy organizations permit disagreement, outside relationships, evidence-based correction, and meaningful exit. Cult-cartel systems make leaving economically, socially, spiritually, or physically expensive.

And it operates “around” the formal organization too: family members, clients, officials, admirers, intimidated neighbors, and dependent businesses can become an outer ring without officially belonging. The cartel controls supply; the surrounding cult controls the story explaining why that control is rightful, inevitable, or untouchable.

Cartel captures the channel. Cult captures the interpreter. The strongest predatory systems capture both.